          { name: "Property Tax Fairness and Fixed-Cost Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 89.1% homeownership and $96,000 median household income, Addison-2 voters are asset-rich but potentially income-constrained relative to their cost obligations — rising property values often produce tax burden pressure even for affluent households. Property tax fairness, assessment methodology, and state tax policy's impact on homeowning families are the bread-and-butter governance issues for this constituency. A challenger who positions Conlon as insufficiently attentive to property tax concerns among homeowners has a concrete issue that resonates across the district's dominant demographic." },
          { name: "Minority Community and Younger Adult Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "The 8.35% non-white population and median age of 49.1 together suggest a constituency that includes meaningful numbers of younger adults and minority residents whose economic priorities may differ from affluent aging homeowners. Healthcare access, housing affordability, and economic opportunity for younger or minority constituents can become secondary in a representative's agenda if the dominant narrative focuses on property tax and fixed-income concerns. Conlon's 56% margin leaves these communities potentially underrepresented in her electoral coalition and available as swing voters for a challenger who articulates specific commitment to their economic mobility." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as an Insurmountable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in an affluent homeowner district with high college attainment is not a commanding mandate — it is a modest majority of a contested electorate. In a district where 44% actively opposed the incumbent in 2024 and material cost-of-living concerns are rising, any challenger who articulates a coherent message on healthcare and tax fairness can shift four to five points and flip the seat. The demographic stability of the district (89% homeowners, 91% white) should not be mistaken for electoral stability; affluent homeowners are mobile voters who respond to specific governance concerns." },
          { name: "Assuming Inherited Democratic Loyalty Among Affluent Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "White, affluent, college-educated homeowners in prosperous rural Vermont communities are not reliably Democratic despite the current partisan alignment. These voters are sensitive to tax burden, healthcare costs, and whether government is protecting their economic interests — concerns that can shift them toward a credible centrist or reform-oriented challenger if they perceive the incumbent as inattentive or ideologically misaligned with their material concerns. Conlon should not campaign this seat as though affluent homeowner support is uncontestable; it is contingent on perceived attentiveness to their specific economic priorities." }
