          { name: "Healthcare System Integrity & Long-Term Care Economics", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.5 median age with substantial household assets and professional income, this constituency is planning for healthcare costs and long-term care expenses with concrete financial urgency. Rising insurance premiums, rural hospital viability, skilled nursing facility affordability, and whether state Medicaid policy supports or undermines healthcare access are material governance issues for an aging, educated, affluent constituency that will evaluate candidates on whether they understand the economics of healthcare in Vermont." },
          { name: "Why the Plurality Chose North and Birong Over Partisan Alternatives", tag: "careful", why: "The 28% Republican + 25% Democratic result, with 47% consolidating behind North and Birong, is not a statistical artifact or a base-consolidation challenge — it is a conscious rejection of both major parties by nearly half a district of educated, affluent homeowners. A candidate who wants to win this seat must understand specifically what drove those voters away from partisan options: fiscal concerns unaddressed by either party, institutional governance preferences that don't align with either major-party platform, or specific local issues where both parties failed to deliver. Without this diagnosis, any candidate will be campaigning to consolidate the same 25-28% that just lost." },
          { name: "Partisan Tribal Loyalty as a Vote-Winning Assumption", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 43% hold college degrees, $95,931 is the median household income, and nearly half chose non-major-party alternatives in 2024, assuming that partisan identity alone will produce wins is strategically incoherent. This is a constituency sophisticated enough to evaluate on policy specifics and outcomes; candidates who default to partisan messaging or abstract conservative/progressive positioning without evidence-based detail will find this educated electorate skeptical and unmoved." },
          { name: "Treating 25-28% as a Stable Base Without Addressing What Made 47% Leave", tag: "avoid", why: "The North and Birong plurality represents an active defection from both major parties in a district with high education and income levels — populations that traditionally consolidate around partisan options when offered competent governance. A candidate who campaigns to secure the remaining 25-28% Republican or Democratic base without acknowledging or addressing the specific governance failures or policy gaps that drove 47% to alternatives will find that base is vulnerable to a future challenger who does engage those constituencies with credibility." }
