          { name: "Younger Adult Economic Mobility & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 36.0-year median age, 23.1% renter rate, and $57,308 median household income, Rutland-3 is a district of younger households facing genuine economic pressure. Rents and housing costs in Vermont are rising faster than wages at this income level, creating material economic anxiety for the plurality of residents who rent or have limited equity. A legislative agenda that prioritizes affordable housing development, renter protections, and wage growth policies will address the actual lived experience of the district's demographic majority and prevent future opponents from exploiting this vulnerability." },
          { name: "Service-Sector & Working-Family Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The median household income of $57,308 with 37% college attainment suggests a constituency where college-educated service-sector workers, healthcare employees, educators, and trades people are present but economically constrained relative to credential and experience. Childcare costs, healthcare affordability, retirement security for workers who lack pension coverage, and the wage trajectory in service-sector employment are the material economic concerns of this demographic. Specific policy attention to these working-family economic issues — beyond general appeals to education and opportunity — is necessary to maintain Democratic performance and prevent economic messaging from an opposition candidate." },
          { name: "Demographic Invisibility & Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "The approximately 2% demographic gap — likely American Indian, Alaska Native, or other residents — is small but worth active constituent engagement in Vermont, where indigenous community visibility and economic participation in state politics has increased. While the percentage is not large enough to drive electoral outcomes, treating this constituency as non-existent or invisible creates unnecessary friction with a community whose economic and governance concerns (housing access, healthcare, education equity) parallel those of the broader younger-household constituency and deserve direct attention." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Durable Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100-0 unopposed result reflects either opponent recruitment failure or strategic non-engagement, not genuine voter consolidation or satisfaction — particularly in a district with this income level, age profile, and economic pressures. An incumbent who assumes the 2024 unopposed margin means the seat is safe is making a strategic error; a future Republican candidate who campaigns on housing affordability, wage growth, and economic opportunity for younger households in Rutland-3 will find the district substantially more competitive than the unopposed result suggests. This seat requires active constituent engagement to remain secure." },
          { name: "Assuming College Attainment (37%) Means Economic Security or Professional-Class Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "The 37% college attainment rate is meaningful but does not translate into a college-educated professional constituency with economic security or predictable partisan loyalty. At $57,308 median income, many college-educated residents in this district face underemployment, wage stagnation, or service-sector employment that does not match their credential expectations. Messaging that assumes college-educated voters are politically stable or economically satisfied will miss the actual economic frustration of a significant portion of the district and will leave room for an opponent who speaks to the gap between education and economic reality." }
