          { name: "Professional & Educated Constituency Material Concerns: Healthcare, Education, Property Taxes", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $99,824 median income, 64.5% college attainment, and 44.1 median age, this is a constituency of established professionals and educators whose economic stakes are in healthcare costs, education funding quality, and property tax management — not abstract ideology. The absence of Republican viability suggests this electorate has concluded neither national Republican positioning nor generic conservative governance addresses their material concerns; a winning candidate of any party must articulate specific, concrete solutions to these three issues that resonate with professionals who have the resources and education to evaluate claims critically." },
          { name: "The Meaning of 0% Republican Performance in a Wealthy, Educated District", tag: "careful", why: "A 0% Republican result in a district with the highest median income and second-highest college attainment in Vermont is not a statistical accident or the result of low Republican turnout — it reflects a genuine misalignment between Republican positioning and this electorate's actual preferences. Whether driven by social policy disagreement, environmental values, or assessment that Republicans do not address this constituency's material concerns, the zero represents an active rejection, not indifference. For a Republican candidate, this means entry into the race requires either a credible alternative Republican profile fundamentally different from national party positioning, or honest assessment that this district is not winnable under current conditions." },
          { name: "Assuming the 39%-37% Margin is Stable or Reflects Democratic Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "A 2-point margin in a 76%-consolidated electorate is not a comfortable incumbent advantage; it is a signal of genuine fluidity between two alternatives that voters regard as roughly equivalent options. Holcombe should not campaign as though 39% is an incumbent floor — a credible Independent or moderate Republican who can consolidate opposition voters and persuade swing voters that the incumbent has lost touch with district concerns could plausibly win. Any Democratic strategy that treats this result as a secure base without active engagement of the Masland opposition will risk losing a seat that remains genuinely competitive." },
          { name: "Dismissing Independent Candidates or Treating Masland's 37% as a Spoiler Threat", tag: "avoid", why: "Masland's 37% result in a two-candidate race suggests the Independent candidacy reflects genuine constituent preference, not protest voting or strategic positioning — and the 17.8% renter rate means professional households without deep local roots may be especially receptive to candidate-specific appeals rather than partisan loyalty. For Holcombe, framing Masland or an Independent challenger as a 'spoiler' will likely alienate the educated, civically engaged voters who view Independent representation as a legitimate option. The viable Democratic strategy is to win these voters on material grounds, not to dismiss the choice they have already made as illegitimate." }
