          { name: "Progressive Coalition Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 36-31 split between Harrison and Hashim in a district with no reported Republican margin means the entire election hinges on which progressive formulation consolidates the 67% combined base. At 42.6% college-educated and $66,786 income, Windham voters evaluate on policy substance and governance vision — not partisan identity. Identifying specifically what drove support to each candidate in 2024 and building a platform that synthesizes rather than replaces either coalition is the fundamental path to majority." },
          { name: "Education, Environment & Economic Opportunity Specificity", tag: "lean-into", why: "An affluent, highly educated district with 42.6% college attainment and a median age of 46.6 expects candidates to speak with precision about how specific policies translate to local outcomes — not abstract progressive values. Environmental policy, housing affordability, rural broadband, and the economics of the knowledge sector in southeastern Vermont are the material governance concerns that differentiate strong progressive candidates from weak ones in a district this educated. Vague commitments to 'climate action' or 'housing solutions' will not move voters who have already heard them from multiple candidates." },
          { name: "Renter vs. Homeowner Coalition Building", tag: "careful", why: "At 27.7% renter rate and 42.6% college-educated, Windham has meaningful segments of younger educated professionals and established homeowners — two cohorts whose policy priorities on housing, transportation, and service delivery can diverge. Younger renters often prioritize housing affordability and public transit; established homeowners prioritize property tax stability and local control. Any candidate must address both constituencies' material concerns without allowing perceived prioritization of one group over the other to splinter the progressive base." },
          { name: "Treating the Margin as Stable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 36-31 split is not a durable outcome — it represents a coalition that fractured or failed to consolidate in 2024. Any candidate who campaigns as though one of these candidates' bases is 'locked in' will discover at election time that 30-35% is a ceiling, not a floor, when the other progressive camp organizes an alternative or sits out. The 28% spread between first and last place among progressives suggests real vulnerability to losing either constituency to a better-articulated vision." },
          { name: "Assuming the Absence of Conservative Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "The lack of reported Republican margin may reflect genuine absence of Republican voters, or it may reflect that they are below reporting threshold, voted conditionally for a specific candidate, or are present but disengaged. Building a campaign that explicitly repels socially moderate, fiscally conservative voters — even if they are a small minority — risks losing the last 5-10% that might be available in a race where the primary margin is only five points. In a district this close among progressives, losing even modest conservative support to abstention or a write-in costs credibility." }
